{#
  Inbox preheader — the grey line the client shows after the subject.

  MJML's <mj-preview> emits the hidden div, but nothing more: if the preview text is shorter than
  the strip the client has room for, the client keeps reading INTO the body and appends whatever
  comes first. That is why the supplier email used to read:

      "No listing fee. Reach qualified buyers … Dear {{company_name}} I'm {{sender_name}} …"

  The fix is the standard one — pad the hidden div out past the longest preview strip (~140 chars in
  Gmail) with a run of zero-width non-joiner + non-breaking space. The pair is deliberate: the
  zero-width character occupies no visual space, and the &nbsp; between them stops clients from
  collapsing the run. Invisible everywhere, and it truncates the leak instead of the copy.

  Included from each email's <mj-head> INSTEAD of writing <mj-preview> there, so no template can
  forget the padding. `content.document.preview` stays the single source of the text.
#}
{# (`range()` loop, not `'…' * 60` — that is Jinja2 string repetition; Nunjucks evaluates it to NaN
   and cheerfully writes "NaN" into the preheader.) #}
<mj-preview>{$ content.document.preview $}{% for _ in range(60) %}&zwnj;&nbsp;{% endfor %}</mj-preview>
